  "notes": "Fixed selector 1 to (a) traverse the full assigned route MESSAGE->CONVERSATION->CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP->USER->WORKSPACE_MEMBERSHIP in a single query (messages->channels->channel_members->users->user_teams), and (b) use an explicit message_id scope list because the AND-only filter grammar cannot express the disjunction of the several distinct infra-incident keyword themes (login failures vs. OOM/memory pressure vs. budget overrun vs. CDN-latency outage) needed to separate matches from same-channel non-matches (e.g. the engineering channel's UX note, captcha reminder, joke reply, AWS-pricing question, and vending-machine chatter, which are not incident reports). The scope list is exactly the union of messages_engineering_incident and messages_infra_incident, i.e. the two underspecified Robert alternatives' qualifying message sets, consistent with the union-of-alternatives resolution rule; the focal route/filters confirm each of those messages sits in a channel where a user named Robert holds conversation membership and that user holds workspace membership in T01WORKSPACE, without narrowing scope to only the intended answers independently of the route. On inspecting the full population of Robert Chen's channels, the C_GROWTH 'Tokyo launch' CDN-latency thread was judged to satisfy 'infrastructure incident' (an outage-like performance incident) and is included in messages_infra_incident; C_MODEL (model-quality/hallucination) and C_FRONTEND (React hydration slowness) content was judged non-infrastructure and excluded. Obligation 2's selector (rooted at user_teams) returns both Robert Walsh's admin row and Robert Chen's member row, per the exposure qualification's instruction not to collapse the two candidates into one. Access note: the presumed actor (U01AGENBOT9) lacks conversation membership in C_GROWTH (product-growth); reading that channel's history for the Robert-Chen alternative is not established under documented APIs and remains a genuine access limitation, not a fabricated fact."
